      "solution": "$\\int \\frac{\\sin 2x}{(3-\\cos 2x)^3}\\,dx = \\int \\frac{1}{u^3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{du}{dx}\\right)dx\\quad \\left\\{u = 3-\\cos 2x \\implies \\frac{du}{dx} = 2\\sin 2x\\right\\} = \\int \\frac{1}{2}u^{-3}\\,du = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}u^{-2}\\right) + c = -\\frac{1}{4u^2} + c = -\\frac{1}{4(3-\\cos 2x)^2} + c$",
